  "summary": "Two members of Oakland's Privacy Advisory Commission, Brian Hofer and Sean Everhart, resigned, saying the City Council and OPD ignore the commission's recommendations and expose the city to legal risk by failing to comply with privacy laws including SB 34 governing ALPRs. They raised particular concerns about OPD's Flock Safety camera network, OPD's inability to audit Flock data, and alleged sharing of data in violation of sanctuary-city rules.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "Hofer also castigated the City Attorney's Office for, in his view, failing to advise OPD of Senate Bill 34, the state law imposing restrictions on automated license plate reader, or ALPR, systems.",
      "paragraph_idx": 15
    },
    {
      "quote": "At the Privacy Advisory Commission's meeting on Oct. 2, an Oakland police representative told commissioners that OPD has no means of auditing the system's data collection.",
      "paragraph_idx": 22
    },
    {
      "quote": "They have zero remorse. They don't care that the Trump administration is hunting people down, that red states are hunting people down. They're just sending all this data over illegally.",
      "paragraph_idx": 19
    }
  ],
